"description": "Produce an article-ready factual overview of the Ring of 
Nestor, including its date, material, findspot, discovery context, iconography 
or inscriptions if applicable, current repository, and the main scholarly 
interpretations or disputes.", "weight": 1.0, "requirement": "must", 
"low_gain_streak": 0, "supported_boundary": "The evidence establishes that the 
Ring of Nestor is a Mycenaean gold signet ring, catalogued in the Corpus der 
Minoischen und Mykenischen Siegel as CMS VI no. 277 and discussed by Theodore 
Eliopoulos (2013) and A. G. Vlachopoulos (2020) in the context of Aegean 
iconography and authenticity debates. It is dated to the early Late Bronze Age 
(16th–15th century BC, about 3,500 years ago). The ring is described as 
unusually large and heavy among Aegean signet rings, with approximate 
dimensions around 3.3–3.5 cm by about 5.7 cm and a weight cited near 82.95 g, 
though secondary summaries conflict on its exact measurements and ranking among
the largest rings. One strand of evidence associates the ring with CMS I, 179, 
reportedly unearthed in December 1915 at Tiryns by Apostolos Arvanitopoulos and
described as a Mycenaean gold signet ring; this is presented as its discovery 
context and archaeological provenance in the summaries, but relies on 
platform-generated text rather than directly quoted primary catalog data. The 
iconography is characterized as an elaborate and populous glyptic scene 
organized around a guardian figure beside an old world tree; Vlachopoulos and 
Eliopoulos highlight this as a key pictorial unit, with Vlachopoulos 
re-identifying Evans’s ‘watch-dog’ or ‘dragon’ motif as a scorpion functioning 
as a major apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean imagery, and using this to 
provide a coherent interpretation of the scene. Secondary summaries also 
mention proposals that the engraved scene may include a female deity (possibly 
Demeter), an eagle of Zeus, and symbols of the four seasons, sun, and moon, but
these interpretive details are drawn from lower-confidence, derivative 
summaries rather than clearly cited scholarly passages. The ring is repeatedly 
treated as a focus for debates over authenticity: earlier 20th‑century 
scholars, especially in the 1920s, sometimes regarded the Ring of Nestor and 
the Ring of Minos as modern forgeries because of obscure or ‘legendary’ 
discovery circumstances, while Eliopoulos and Vlachopoulos explicitly document 
and argue for its authenticity, emphasizing iconographic comparison, links to 
incurved altar imagery, and the support provided by recently discovered 
comparable wall paintings, rings, figurines, and ivories and by closer study of
Late Bronze Age glyptic techniques. The ring’s extraordinary craftsmanship and 
microscopic engravings are noted, and one summary characterizes it as a 
κειμήλιο (heirloom) within Mycenaean culture, though that cultural-function 
claim is lower confidence. No firm, directly evidenced statement is provided 
about the ring’s current repository or holding institution.", 
"last_evaluated_successful_rounds": 1, "text": "Produce an article-ready 
