commit_report_chapter_batch({'chapters': [{'chapter_id': 1, 'bundle_token': 
'b1-c81c4a4f', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is the conventional name given to
an elaborately engraved Mycenaean gold signet ring catalogued in the Corpus der
Minoischen und Mykenischen Siegel (CMS) as CMS I, 179 and CMS VI, no. 
277.[1][4] It is one of the largest known Aegean signet rings, weighing about 
82.95 g and measuring roughly several centimetres across the bezel,[1] and 
carries a densely composed ritual scene that has attracted sustained scholarly 
attention for its complex iconography and for the questions it raises about 
authenticity and forgery in Aegean glyptic art.[3][4] The ring is traditionally
associated with the Palace of Nestor at Pylos and with finds from Tiryns in the
Argolid, situating it within the Late Bronze Age palatial environment of 
Mycenaean Greece.[1][2]\n\nSince its emergence from early twentieth‑century 
excavations, the Ring of Nestor has been central to debates about the 
genuineness of certain Creto‑Mycenaean gold rings, some of which were once 
suspected to be modern creations because of the obscure circumstances of their 
discovery.[3][4] Theodore Eliopoulos and later A. G. Vlachopoulos have argued, 
on the basis of iconographic comparison and technical study, that the Ring of 
Nestor belongs to an early phase of the Late Bronze Age (16th–15th century BC) 
and should be regarded as authentic, alongside the better‑known Ring of 
Minos.[4] At the same time, platform summaries of recent scholarship highlight 
a tension between the ring’s close parallels with Bronze Age motifs, such as 
incurved altars, and suggestions that aspects of its imagery may echo cultural 
practices of the 1920s, a tension that keeps questions of authenticity open in 
some discussions.[3]\n\nThe ring’s imagery has been read as a detailed cultic 
composition that may allude to deities and cosmological cycles, including a 
female figure sometimes compared with Demeter, a bird associated with Zeus, and
recurring elements linked to the seasons framed by solar and lunar 
symbols.[1][2] More recent work has focused on the identification of a key 
guardian figure on the ring, previously described as a dog or dragon, as a 
scorpion functioning as an apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean art.[4] These
interpretations remain hypothetical and are constrained by the limited 
archaeological and textual evidence for Mycenaean religion in the LH II and 
Middle Helladic periods, but they illustrate the importance of the Ring of 
Nestor in reconstructions of Mycenaean cult, heirloom objects, and elite 
identity.[1][3]\n\n', 'evidence_refs': ['e1', 'e2', 'e3', 'e4', 'e5', 'e6', 
'e7', 'e8', 'e9', 'e10', 'e11', 'e12', 'e13', 'e14', 'e15', 'e16', 'e17', 
'e18', 'e19', 'e20', 'e21', 'e22'], 'disclosed_conflict_refs': ['x1']}]})
