Ring of Nestor\n\nThe Ring of Nestor is the conventional name given to an 
elaborately engraved Mycenaean gold signet ring catalogued in the Corpus der 
Minoischen und Mykenischen Siegel (CMS) as CMS I, 179 and CMS VI, no. 
277.[1][4] It is one of the largest known Aegean signet rings, weighing about 
82.95 g and measuring roughly several centimetres across the bezel,[1] and 
carries a densely composed ritual scene that has attracted sustained scholarly 
attention for its complex iconography and for the questions it raises about 
authenticity and forgery in Aegean glyptic art.[3][4] The ring is traditionally
associated with the Palace of Nestor at Pylos and with finds from Tiryns in the
Argolid, situating it within the Late Bronze Age palatial environment of 
Mycenaean Greece.[1][2]\n\nSince its emergence from early twentieth‑century 
excavations, the Ring of Nestor has been central to debates about the 
genuineness of certain Creto‑Mycenaean gold rings, some of which were once 
suspected to be modern creations because of the obscure circumstances of their 
discovery.[3][4] Theodore Eliopoulos and later A. G. Vlachopoulos have argued, 
on the basis of iconographic comparison and technical study, that the Ring of 
Nestor belongs to an early phase of the Late Bronze Age (16th–15th century BC) 
and should be regarded as authentic, alongside the better‑known Ring of 
Minos.[4] At the same time, platform summaries of recent scholarship highlight 
a tension between the ring’s close parallels with Bronze Age motifs, such as 
incurved altars, and suggestions that aspects of its imagery may echo cultural 
practices of the 1920s, a tension that keeps questions of authenticity open in 
some discussions.[3]\n\nThe ring’s imagery has been read as a detailed cultic 
composition that may allude to deities and cosmological cycles, including a 
female figure sometimes compared with Demeter, a bird associated with Zeus, and
recurring elements linked to the seasons framed by solar and lunar 
symbols.[1][2] More recent work has focused on the identification of a key 
guardian figure on the ring, previously described as a dog or dragon, as a 
scorpion functioning as an apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean art.[4] These
interpretations remain hypothetical and are constrained by the limited 
archaeological and textual evidence for Mycenaean religion in the LH II and 
Middle Helladic periods, but they illustrate the importance of the Ring of 
Nestor in reconstructions of Mycenaean cult, heirloom objects, and elite 
identity.[1][3]\n\n## Discovery and Provenance\n\nThe Ring of Nestor entered 
scholarly discussion through early twentieth‑century excavations in the Argolid
and subsequent attributions within the Corpus der Minoischen und Mykenischen 
Siegel. Platform summaries of a study on CMS I, 179 report that the ring was 
unearthed in December 1915 at Tiryns by the Greek archaeologist Apostolos 
Arvanitopoulos, and describe it as a Mycenaean gold signet ring.[1] In this 
account, the ring’s large size and weight—around 3.5 × 5.7 cm across the bezel 
with an annulus diameter of about 2.2 cm and a mass of 82.95 g—are highlighted 
as distinguishing features, and the piece is characterized as the largest 
Mycenaean signet ring known in the CMS corpus.[1] These details situate the 
object within the wider assemblage of Late Bronze Age elite jewelry and sealing
devices from the Argolid and Messenia, which are commonly grouped under the 
label of Creto‑Mycenaean gold rings.[1][4]\n\nThe conventional association of 
the ring with the Palace of Nestor at Pylos rests partly on its Mycenaean 
stylistic features and partly on later popular and scholarly usage linking 
elaborate signet rings with the Homeric figure of Nestor and the Pylian 
palatial centre.[2][4] A separate news report describes the discovery of a gold
signet ring with a detailed ritual‑sacrifice scene near the Palace of Nestor in
Pylos and dates that find to around 3,500 years before the present, within the 
Mycenaean period.[2] The relationship between this Pylos ring and the 
CMS‑catalogued Ring of Nestor is not made explicit in the available summaries, 
and it is possible that the label \"Ring of Nestor\" has been applied to more 
than one Mycenaean ring in modern reception.[2][4]\n\nFrom the 1920s onward, 
the Ring of Nestor became involved in a broader debate over the authenticity of
certain Creto‑Mycenaean gold signet rings, including the so‑called Ring of 
Minos.[3][4] Some early twentieth‑century scholars, reacting to the unclear or 
\"legendary\" circumstances under which several large gold rings were acquired 
or reported, regarded them as modern forgeries rather than Bronze Age 
originals.[3][4] Theodore Eliopoulos’s 2013 article in the Annuario della 
Scuola Archeologica di Atene e delle Missioni Italiane in Oriente situates the 
Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 277) within this discourse on Aegean forgeries and 
undertakes a detailed iconographic analysis and comparison with securely 
provenanced material.[3] According to the article’s abstract and platform 
summaries, Eliopoulos’s long‑term research program seeks to document the ring’s
authenticity and to reassess cases in which Creto‑Mycenaean gold rings were 
erroneously identified as fakes, concluding in favour of the authenticity of 
both Nestor’s and Minos’s rings.[3][4]\n\nA. G. Vlachopoulos’s 2020 
contribution \"The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity\" in the 
volume *Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography* further 
develops this reassessment.[4] In his abstract, Vlachopoulos states that the 
tendency to treat the Ring of Nestor and related pieces as forgeries has been 
completely reconsidered in favour of their authenticity and that this revision 
is reinforced by recent discoveries of iconographically comparable 
material—such as wall paintings, other gold rings, figurines and ivories—as 
well as by more detailed study of the techniques and craftsmanship of early 
Late Bronze Age signet rings dated to the 16th–15th centuries BC.[4] 
