{'chapters': [{'chapter_id': 1, 'bundle_token': 'b1-c81c4a4f', 'content': 'The 
Ring of Nestor is the conventional name given to an elaborately engraved 
Mycenaean gold signet ring catalogued in the Corpus der Minoischen und 
Mykenischen Siegel (CMS) as CMS I, 179 and CMS VI, no. 277.[1][4] It is one of 
the largest known Aegean signet rings, weighing about 82.95 g and measuring 
roughly several centimetres across the bezel,[1] and carries a densely composed
ritual scene that has attracted sustained scholarly attention for its complex 
iconography and for the questions it raises about authenticity and forgery in 
Aegean glyptic art.[3][4] The ring is traditionally associated with the Palace 
of Nestor at Pylos and with finds from Tiryns in the Argolid, situating it 
within the Late Bronze Age palatial environment of Mycenaean 
Greece.[1][2]\n\nSince its emergence from early twentieth‑century excavations, 
the Ring of Nestor has been central to debates about the genuineness of certain
Creto‑Mycenaean gold rings, some of which were once suspected to be modern 
creations because of the obscure circumstances of their discovery.[3][4] 
Theodore Eliopoulos and later A. G. Vlachopoulos have argued, on the basis of 
iconographic comparison and technical study, that the Ring of Nestor belongs to
an early phase of the Late Bronze Age (16th–15th century BC) and should be 
regarded as authentic, alongside the better‑known Ring of Minos.[4] At the same
time, AI‑generated key‑takeaway summaries of Eliopoulos’s work present a 
tension in the way the ring’s imagery is discussed: they state that the 
depiction of incurved altars supports the ring’s authenticity by aligning it 
with securely dated Bronze Age iconography, while also suggesting that aspects 
of its scene may reflect cultural practices from the 1920s.[3] This dual 
presentation underlies a factual conflict in secondary accounts, between 
arguments for the ring’s status as a genuine Aegean Bronze Age object and the 
possibility that parts of its imagery may have been shaped or selectively 
understood through modern cultural frameworks.[3][4]\n\nThe ring’s imagery has 
been read as a detailed cultic composition that may allude to deities and 
cosmological cycles, including a female figure sometimes compared with Demeter,
a bird associated with Zeus, and recurring elements linked to the seasons 
framed by solar and lunar symbols.[1][2] More recent work has focused on the 
identification of a key guardian figure on the ring, previously described as a 
dog or dragon, as a scorpion functioning as an apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age 
Aegean art.[4] These interpretations remain hypothetical and are constrained by
the limited archaeological and textual evidence for Mycenaean religion in the 
LH II and Middle Helladic periods, but they illustrate the importance of the 
Ring of Nestor in reconstructions of Mycenaean cult, heirloom objects, and 
elite identity.[1][3]\n\n', 'evidence_refs': ['e1', 'e2', 'e3', 'e4', 'e5', 
