sustained scholarly attention for its complex iconography and for the questions
it raises about authenticity and forgery in Aegean glyptic art.[3][4] The ring 
is traditionally associated with the Palace of Nestor at Pylos and with finds 
from Tiryns in the Argolid, situating it within the Late Bronze Age palatial 
environment of Mycenaean Greece.[1][2]\n\nSince its emergence from early 
twentieth‑century excavations, the Ring of Nestor has been central to debates 
about the genuineness of certain Creto‑Mycenaean gold rings, some of which were
once suspected to be modern creations because of the obscure circumstances of 
their discovery.[3][4] Theodore Eliopoulos and later A. G. Vlachopoulos have 
argued, on the basis of iconographic comparison and technical study, that the 
